+# KERNEL-GAP P2.2 (docs/47 F5.3): merged-destination apply for multi-shard layers.
+# At prefill the per-shard outputs were materialised and then torch.cat-ed - about
+# 25.7 GB of pure copy traffic per 2048-token chunk (gate|up 17.4 + GDN qkvz|z 6.4 +
+# attn qkv 1.9). hgemm accepts a strided C (ldc = stride(-2), hgemm.cu:41-51), so each
+# shard can write straight into its slice of the merged destination. The per-shard
+# svh hadamard is DEFERRED and replaced by ONE had_r_128 over the merged tensor with
+# the concatenated svh: every shard width is a multiple of 128, so each 128-column
+# hadamard block lies entirely inside one shard and the result is bit-identical to
+# the per-shard transforms. At decode rows the exl3_gemm kernel needs contiguous C,
+# so that branch computes into a temp and copies into the slice - the same bytes the
+# torch.cat moved before, inside the same CUDA graph. Both row-count branches live
+# inside custom ops so the choice stays opaque to Dynamo (same reasoning as
+# vllm::exl3_gemm above).
+
+
